module std.ffi
//! The Lyra half of the C boundary.
//!
//! An `extern` declares what C provides and the compiler checks the call; what it cannot
//! do is make the result *safe*, because C hands back a pointer and nothing else. A raw
//! pointer carries no length, so nothing about it can be checked — `p.offset(n)^` will
//! read whatever is at that address, and `unsafe` is the only thing standing between a
//! program and that fact.
//!
//! **A pointer and a length together can be checked**, and that is the whole idea here:
//! `CBuffer` pairs the two, `get` traps on an index outside them exactly as `xs[i]` does,
//! and `unsafe` appears once — in this file — instead of at every use. It is the same
//! division `random_seed`/`Rng` and `read_line`/`parse_i64` already draw: the compiler
//! provides only what cannot be written in Lyra, and the part that can be is written here.
//!
//! What it does **not** do is make the pointer valid. Nothing can: a `CBuffer` over
//! memory C has freed, or built with a length larger than the allocation, checks against
//! the wrong number and reads out of bounds without complaint. Constructing one is the
//! promise; the checking is what the promise then buys.
//!
//! ```
//! import std.ffi.{ CBuffer }
//!
//! unsafe extern pure strlen: (^u8) -> u64
//!
//! let read_c_string = (p: ^u8) -> CBuffer =>
//!   CBuffer { ptr: p, len: i64(unsafe { strlen(p) }) }
//! ```

/// A foreign buffer: a pointer, and the number of bytes it is valid for.
///
/// Bytes rather than a generic element type, because that is what crosses the boundary —
/// `^u8` is what `lyra-E063` leaves you holding for a string, an array or any other block
/// of memory, and a typed view over it would be a second question (alignment) on top of
/// this one.
///
/// The fields are public because building one *is* the unsafe act, and hiding them behind
/// a constructor would only add a place to look: a caller who can produce the pointer can
/// already produce a wrong length.
pub struct CBuffer {
  /// Where the bytes are. Not owned — this type never frees anything, on the standing
  /// rule that ownership does not cross the boundary in either direction.
  ptr: ^u8,
  /// How many bytes `ptr` is valid for. Whoever built the buffer promised this.
  len: i64,
}

/// The byte at `i`.
///
/// `pure` here means what the compiler means by it — no tracked effect — and **not** that
/// two calls must agree. The bytes belong to someone else, who may write them between one
/// call and the next; that is a property of foreign memory rather than of this function,
/// and no bound could describe it.
///
/// # Panics
/// Traps if `i` is outside the buffer, which is the same answer `xs[i]` gives and the
/// reason this type exists. The message is a constant rather than an interpolation, so
/// `get` allocates nothing and stays callable from `noalloc` code.
pub let get = pure noalloc (self: CBuffer, i: i64) -> u8 => {
  if i < 0 || i >= self.len {
    panic("std.ffi: CBuffer index out of bounds")
  }
  unsafe { self.ptr.offset(i)^ }
}
